<https://wealth.example.org/neowealth/column/main/dim_mf_fund_complete/asset_alloc_equity_net> a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    dcterms:description "Look-through equity weight. The _net suffix implies NET of derivative and short positions, so a value can in principle exceed 100% or go negative. Main analytical use: TRUE asset-allocation look-through - a client's equity exposure is their direct equity PLUS fund_value * asset_alloc_equity_net for every fund held. This is how you reconcile bottom-up allocation against client_aum's splits." ;
    rdfs:domain <https://wealth.example.org/neowealth/table/main/dim_mf_fund_complete> ;
    meta:nullable "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    meta:sourceColumn "asset_alloc_equity_net" ;
    meta:sourceSqlType "FLOAT" .

<https://wealth.example.org/neowealth/column/main/dim_mf_fund_complete/benchmark_name> a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    dcterms:description "Intended link to dim_cmot_index_prices.index_name by FREE-TEXT MATCH - which, by exact match, finds nothing in this data (see below). NAMING TRAP: dim_product_unified_cat calls this 'benchmark'. The differing names suggest the two were populated by different processes with different conventions, which makes the text join even more fragile. Populated on all 30 rows with 16 distinct values (e.g. 'Nifty 500 TRI' 7, 'Nifty 50 TRI' 3, 'Nifty 100 TRI' 3, 'Nifty Midcap 150 TRI' 3). NONE equals an index_name exactly (0 of 30). It differs from dim_product_unified_cat.benchmark for 19 of the 30 funds (matched on isin). Preserve each label and its unmatched status in stored data and the KG - never strip ' TRI' or store an alias. The ' TRI' strip in 01_domain_and_business_rules.md §3.8 is a QUERY-TIME house rule only, which an equivalent KG query must reproduce exactly as the SQL does." ;
    rdfs:domain <https://wealth.example.org/neowealth/table/main/dim_mf_fund_complete> ;
    meta:nullable "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    meta:sourceColumn "benchmark_name" ;
    meta:sourceSqlType "VARCHAR" .

